row(slug="c2pa", name="C2PA Technical Specification (Content Credentials)", aka=["C2PA", "Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity", "Content Credentials", "C2PA Specification 2.2"],
    url="https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.2/specs/C2PA_Specification.html",
    operator="the Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA), established as a Joint Development Foundation Projects, LLC, Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity Series, with a steering committee (home page)",
    kind="an open technical standard 'for publishers, creators and consumers to establish the origin and edits of digital content', called Content Credentials (home page); the specification describes 'a model for storing and accessing cryptographically verifiable information whose trustworthiness can be assessed based on a defined trust model'",
    areas=["attribution-and-credit", "machine-co-authorship"],
    evidence_kind=["technical standard"],
    unit="a C2PA Manifest (a Content Credential) bound to an asset: assertions, a single claim and a claim signature, made by a claim generator and signed with the signer's credential",
    record_object="the provenance of digital assets (images, video, audio, fonts, documents, among them PDF): how an asset was created and changed and by which actors, including generative AI output, recorded as assertions about the asset",
    record={"keeps": "not authorship: the manifest keeps signed assertions about an asset's creation and edits; the trust model rests on 'the identity of the signer', the credential holder of the signing key",
            "name": "not recorded as an author's name: the signer is 'identified by the subject of the credential', and a Valid manifest's claim 'can be attributed to the claim generator'; the standard c2pa.metadata assertion supports only a subset of Dublin Core (dc:coverage, dc:date, dc:format, dc:identifier, dc:language, dc:relation, dc:type), the schema.org assertion is deprecated, and the 'actors' field was removed in version 2.0; custom metadata assertions 'can contain any values from any schemas'",
            "holder": "the asset carries the manifest (embedded or external); the signer holds the private key; validators keep trust lists, including the C2PA Trust List; the specification is under CC BY 4.0",
            "function": "attribution of assertions to the signer: 'All created_assertions are attributed to the signer', while gathered assertions, such as 'information entered by a human actor', are 'not attributed to the signer'; actions record what was done and by which software or hardware (softwareAgent), and a c2pa.created action carries a digitalSourceType (trainedAlgorithmicMedia for generative AI output); the specification makes no judgment on whether the assertions are true, which is left to the consumer"},
    method="a claim generator (hardware or software) assembles assertions, binds them to the asset's content by cryptographic hashes or soft bindings, and signs the claim with the signer's credential; a validator checks the assertions, ingredients, time-stamp, revocation, signature and content, and reports the manifest Well-Formed, Valid or Trusted",
    coverage={"geography": "global by design ('Global Universality')", "period": "specification 2.2 (May 2025), with versions 1.0 to 2.5 listed on the specification site"},
    count={"value": "not stated in the saved sources (no count of adopters or credentialed assets)", "as_of": "2026-11-01"},
    holdings=["the C2PA Technical Specification 2.2 (read here) and its version list from 1.0 to 2.5", "the trust model and three manifest states: Well-Formed, Valid, Trusted", "standard assertions, among them actions, ingredients, metadata, thumbnails, hashes and time-stamps", "digitalSourceType values recorded with c2pa.created, including trainedAlgorithmicMedia for generative AI output"],
    stated_limits=["'C2PA specifications SHOULD NOT provide value judgments about whether a given set of provenance data is 'good' or 'bad,' merely whether the assertions included within can be validated as associated with the underlying asset, correctly formed, and free from tampering'", "the consumer 'is not specified in the trust model' and uses the signer's identity, with other trust signals, 'to decide whether the assertions made about an asset are true'", "gathered assertions, including information entered by a human actor, are 'not attributed to the signer'", "adoption is 'opt-in'"],
    answers=["whether a manifest's assertions are bound to an asset, correctly formed and untampered since signing (Well-Formed, Valid)", "whether the signing credential chains to a trust anchor the validator accepts (Trusted)", "which actions a manifest records, with which software agents, and whether a c2pa.created action declares generative AI as the digital source type", "to whom a Valid manifest's claim can be attributed: the claim generator"],
    does_not_answer=["who authored a work: validation establishes association, form and freedom from tampering, and attributes the claim to a claim generator and the assertions to a signer", "whether a signed assertion is true (left to the consumer)", "whether a human statement of authorship is correct: such entries are gathered and are not attributed to the signer", "anything about an asset that carries no manifest", "how an authorial name, pseudonym or heteronym is held: the standard metadata assertion supports no Dublin Core creator field"],
    ls_reading="C2PA authenticates a chain of custody for assertions: a validated manifest establishes that signed assertions are associated with the asset, well formed and untampered, and attributes them to a signer and a claim generator; it is no determination of authorship, and the specification refuses value judgments on the assertions. Against #940 its responsibility layer is the signer's credential, and a human's own account of what was made is gathered and left unattributed, so the standard carries provenance of process and has no field for the authorial function or a chosen name.",
    host_relation="infrastructure",
    record_type="standard",
    question="Are the signed assertions about an asset's creation and edits bound to it, well formed and untampered, and whose credential signed them (provenance validation; it is no test of authorship)?",
    data_access="the specification is public on spec.c2pa.org, with explainer, implementer guidance, CDDL and JSON schemas listed; the trust list and implementations are not in the saved sources",
    license="'This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License' (specification); the site UI under MPL-2.0",
    lifecycle={"state": "active", "detail": "version 2.2 dated May 2025 is the one read; the specification site lists versions through 2.5 and technical specifications 2.3; the site footer reads 'Copyright © 2026'"},
    version_and_time={"spec_read": "2.2 (May 2025)", "versions_listed": "1.0 to 2.5", "read": "2026-11-01"},
    negative_tags=["NOT a determination of authorship: validating signed assertions establishes association, form and freedom from tampering, and makes no value judgment on them (the selection review's point, borne out by §1.2 and §14)", "NOT a detector of AI content: it records what a claim generator declares, such as a digitalSourceType", "NOT a record of authorial names: the standard metadata assertion omits a Dublin Core creator field, the schema.org assertion is deprecated and the actors field was removed in 2.0", "NOT a statement of who or what can be an author: it is a technical standard for provenance data", "NOT applicable to assets without a manifest: adoption is opt-in"],
    evidence={
      "home_self_description": Q("c2pa--home", "The Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity, or C2PA, provides an open technical standard for publishers, creators and consumers to establish the origin and edits of digital content. It's called Content Credentials, and it ensures content complies with standards as the digital ecosystem evolves."),
      "nutrition_label": Q("c2pa--home", "Content Credentials function like a nutrition label for digital content, giving a peek at the content's history available for anyone to access, at any time."),
      "operator": Q("c2pa--home", "Copyright © Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity, established as a Joint Development Foundation Projects, LLC, Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity Series."),
      "steering": Q("c2pa--home", "Meet the Steering Committee members"),
      "provenance_purpose": Q("c2pa--spec", "Provenance empowers content creators and editors, regardless of their geographic location or degree of access to technology, to disclose information about how an asset was created, how it was changed and what was changed."),
      "opt_in": Q("c2pa--spec", "It is designed to enable global, opt-in, adoption of digital provenance techniques"),
      "regulators": Q("c2pa--spec", "It is also possible that regulatory bodies and governmental agencies could utilize this specification to establish standards for digital provenance."),
      "scope": Q("c2pa--spec", "This specification describes the technical aspects of the C2PA architecture; a model for storing and accessing cryptographically verifiable information whose trustworthiness can be assessed based on a defined trust model ."),
      "no_value_judgment": Q("c2pa--spec", "C2PA specifications SHOULD NOT provide value judgments about whether a given set of provenance data is 'good' or 'bad,' merely whether the assertions included within can be validated as associated with the underlying asset, correctly formed, and free from tampering."),
      "assertions": Q("c2pa--spec", "These statements, called assertions, make up the provenance of a given asset and represent a series of trust signals that can be used by a human to improve their view of trustworthiness concerning the asset."),
      "claim_signed": Q("c2pa--spec", "This claim is digitally signed by the claim generator on behalf of the signer , using the signer's signing credential, producing the claim signature."),
      "trust_basis": Q("c2pa--spec", "The basis of making trust decisions in C2PA, our trust model , is the identity of the signer associated with the cryptographic signing key used to sign a claim in a C2PA Manifest."),
      "actor": Q("c2pa--spec", "A human or non-human (hardware or software) that is participating in the C2PA ecosystem. For example: a camera (capture device), image editing software, cloud service or the person using such tools."),
      "claim_generator": Q("c2pa--spec", "The non-human (hardware or software) actor that generates the claim about an asset as well as the claim signature , thus leading to the asset 's associated C2PA Manifest ."),
      "signer": Q("c2pa--spec", "The credential holder of a private key that is used to sign the claim . The signer is identified by the subject of the credential."),
      "assertion_def": Q("c2pa--spec", "A data structure which represents a statement either made (or \"created\") by the signer or simply gathered at claim generation-time, concerning the asset ."),
      "authenticity_def": Q("c2pa--spec", "A property of digital content comprising a set of facts (such as the provenance data and hard bindings ) that can be cryptographically verified as not having been tampered with."),
      "created_attributed": Q("c2pa--spec", "All created_assertions are attributed to the signer as the Trust Model is rooted in the trust of the signer."),
      "gathered_not_attributed": Q("c2pa--spec", "By putting an assertion into this list, the claim generator is declaring that the assertion is part of the claim, but it was not sourced from the claim generator and is not attributed to the signer. For example, assertions containing information entered by a human actor would be listed in gathered_assertions ."),
      "consumer_decides": Q("c2pa--spec", "In dashed lines, below, is the consumer (who is not specified in the trust model), who uses the identity of the signer, along with other trust signals, to decide whether the assertions made about an asset are true."),
      "validator_conclusions": Q("c2pa--spec", "The actor consuming the asset, usually through their user agent and its user interface, then has to interpret those statements to arrive at a set of conclusions of their own about the provenance of the asset they are consuming."),
      "manifest_states": Q("c2pa--spec", "Any Trusted manifest is also Valid, and any Valid manifest is also Well-Formed."),
      "valid_attributed": Q("c2pa--spec", "If a C2PA Manifest is Valid, then the manifest's claim can be attributed to the claim generator which is identified by the claim_generator_info field of the claim"),
      "display_warning": Q("c2pa--spec", "a warning that the data shall not be attributed to the manifest's signer,"),
      "metadata_attribution": Q("c2pa--spec", "Having the metadata in an assertion establishes that the metadata in that assertion is significant, because it has been explicitly included in the C2PA Manifest, and signed by a specific signer - thus enabling cryptographic validation and attribution of the data."),
      "custom_metadata": Q("c2pa--spec", "Custom labelled metadata assertions can contain any values from any schemas."),
      "dc_subset": Q("c2pa--spec", "Only the following Dublin Core ( dc ) properties are supported: dc:coverage dc:date dc:format dc:identifier dc:language dc:relation dc:type"),
      "actors_removed": Q("c2pa--spec", "; NOTE: an earlier version of this specification also included an \"actors\" field, however this was removed in version 2.0."),
      "schema_org_deprecated": Q("c2pa--spec", "stds.schema-org Assertion DEPRECATED DEPRECATED DEPRECATED DEPRECATED"),
      "human_entry": Q("c2pa--spec", "humanEntry Assertion contents were entered by a human"),
      "software_agent": Q("c2pa--spec", "The software or hardware used to perform the action can be identified via the softwareAgent field."),
      "digital_source_type": Q("c2pa--spec", "For all assets, a corresponding digitalSourceType field, with an appropriate value, shall be recorded with the c2pa.created action, to indicate the nature of the asset at its inception."),
      "genai_example": Q("c2pa--spec", "EXAMPLE: A generative AI model generates a video in response to a text prompt. The resulting video asset's active manifest would have a c2pa.actions assertion starting with a c2pa.created action, itself having a value of http://cv.iptc.org/newscodes/digitalsourcetype/trainedAlgorithmicMedia in the corresponding digitalSourceType field."),
      "version": Q("c2pa--spec", "5.2.1. 2.2 - May 2025"),
      "signer_terms": Q("c2pa--spec", "Clarified terminology around \"signer\" and \"claim generator\", with respect to their roles"),
      "content_ubiquity": Q("c2pa--spec", "Enable the inclusion of provenance for all common media types, including documents"),
      "license": Q("c2pa--spec", "This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License ."),
      "footer": Q("c2pa--spec", "Copyright © 2026 Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA). Except where noted, the content is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0) license."),
    },
    relations=[],
    unchecked=["the specification beyond the sections read (introduction and scope, glossary, claims §10.2, trust model §14, validation §15.1–15.3, actions §18.14, metadata §18.16, Appendix B and Appendix C); its cryptography, embedding and binding chapters were not read in full", "versions 2.3 to 2.5 (listed and unsaved): later changes to assertions, signers or identity are not recorded here", "the C2PA explainer, UX guidance, harms modelling and AI/ML guidance (linked and unsaved)", "any identity assertion or creator-credential mechanism outside this specification: none appears in the 2.2 text read, and nothing beyond it was saved", "adoption figures, members and the C2PA Trust List", "any legal labelling obligation: none is stated in the saved sources, and the selection review declines to adopt a generic claim about mandatory 2026 labelling"])
